In TypeScript, you can use the "type predication" or "type narrowing" technique to tell the type system that a value may have multiple types in theory but will only have a single specific type in practice. This approach helps the compiler infer the precise type, leading to safer and more efficient code. Here's an example illustrating how to do this:

```typescript
function fetchDataAndPerformAction(url: string): void {
  const data = await fetch(url);

  // Type Predication using a union type
  if (data.status === 200) {
    // Type predicate: assert that the data is an object (instead of a null/undefined value)
    if ("data" in data) {
      processData(data.data as Object);
    }
    // Type predicate alternatively using the "typeof" operator:
    // if ("data" in data && typeof data["data"] === "object") {
    //   processData(data["data"]);
    // }
  } else {
    processError(data.status);
  }
}

function processData(data: Object) {
  console.log(`Processing data of type: ${typeof data}`);
}

function processError(status: number) {
  console.log(`Error occurred with status ${status}`);
}
```

In this example, the `fetchDataAndPerformAction` function fetches data from a given URL. The retrieved data is represented by the variable `data`, which could either be an object (with `data` property) or a null/undefined value, depending on the status code of the request. To help the type system understand that the data will be of one specific type - an object - we use type predication.

The type predication is achieved through the "if" statement and the "in" operator. The "if" statement checks whether the "data" object has a "data" property, which would indicate that the `data.data` property is defined and of type `Object`. If this statement returns true, the type system narrows the type of `data.data` to `Object`, preventing potential errors that might occur when accessing properties of a null/undefined value.

An alternative way to achieve type predication is by using the "typeof" operator. In this case, we check that `typeof data["data"]` is "object", which also asserts that the value at `data["data"]` is an object. The "typeof" operator ensures that the type system considers only the object type in this case.

The `processData` function expects an `Object` as its argument, and the compiler ensures that the correct type is passed based on the type predication. Similarly, in the `processError` function, the status code is used as the argument type, and the compiler guarantees this is represented as a number.

By employing type predication, we can leverage TypeScript's strict type system, ensuring that our code is well-typed, and potential errors are detected at compile time. This approach is especially useful when working with APIs that may return different data types based on certain conditions.

