   ```
2. Define the FEN State:
   Begin by defining the FEN state of the chessboard:
   ```latex
   \begin{document}
   % Define the FEN state
   \def\fen{nrnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR}
   ```
   Replace the `\fen` variable with the actual FEN state you want to visualize.
3. Create a `chessboard` Environment:
   Use the `chessboard` environment to display the chessboard and configure it with specified FEN state:
   ```latex
   \begin{center}
   \begin{chessboard}[fen=\fen]
   \end{chessboard}
   \end{center}
   ```
   The `[fen=\fen]` argument tells the `chessboard` environment to use the defined `\fen` variable for the FEN state.
4. Draw the Board Grid:
   Use TikZ to create a grid overlay on top of the `chessboard` environment. The grid will help display the board in a visually appealing way:
   ```latex
